      "id": "part_01-ch_05",
      "position": 6,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 5",
      "title": "Croagh Patrick: Barefoot Ascent on Ancient Stone in Western Ireland",
      "summary": "Every Reek Sunday in late July, over twenty thousand pilgrims climb the steep quartzite scree of Croagh Patrick in County Mayo, many walking completely barefoot as an act of penance.",
      "content": "![Pilgrims ascending the steep quartzite slopes of Croagh Patrick above Clew Bay](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/croagh-patrick-reek-sunday-barefoot-pilgrims-hero.webp)\n\nTowering 764 meters over the drowned drumlins of Clew Bay in County Mayo, Croagh Patrick—known in Irish as *Cruach Phádraig*—stands as Ireland's holiest mountain and one of Europe's most physically demanding continuous pilgrimage traditions. Long before Christianization, the conical peak served as a Celtic ceremonial site honoring the harvest festival of Lughnasadh. In 441 CE, Saint Patrick reportedly fasted atop its windswept plateau for forty days, banishing snakes and demons into the Atlantic squalls.\n\nOn the final Sunday of July, designated as Reek Sunday, up to twenty-five thousand pilgrims undertake the grueling ascent. The lower slopes of bog and heather give way to the notorious 'cone', a punishing incline of loose quartzite scree where each step can slip backward. In adherence to centuries-old penitential tradition, a dedicated minority of pilgrims climbs completely barefoot, their weathered soles treading across jagged stones and muddy rivulets as a tangible exercise in vulnerability, atonement, and resolve.\n\n![Bare feet walking over rough quartzite stones and mud during Reek Sunday pilgrimage](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/croagh-patrick-reek-sunday-barefoot-pilgrims-detail.webp)\n\nAt the summit plateau, battered by Atlantic mists, stands a solitary concrete chapel erected by local stonemasons in 1905. Here, pilgrims perform *turas*—circumambulating ancient cairns while reciting traditional prayers before attending Mass delivered in both Irish and English. When the squalls part, revealing the shimmering archipelago of Clew Bay below, the arduous physical ordeal transforms into an exhilarating celebration of communal solidarity, grit, and enduring Celtic heritage."
