      "title": "The Goroka Show: Sing-Sing, Bird-of-Paradise Feathers and Body Paint in Papua New Guinea",
      "summary": "In the misty highlands of Papua New Guinea, over a hundred tribes gather for the annual Goroka Show, beating hourglass kundu drums and displaying magnificent headdresses adorned with bird-of-paradise plumes.",
      "content": "![Highland dancers with vibrant bird-of-paradise feather headdresses at Goroka Show](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/goroka-show-sing-sing-papua-new-guinea-hero.webp)\n\nEstablished in the mid-1950s by Australian patrol officers and local mission authorities as a creative means of channeling inter-tribal warfare into peaceful athletic and aesthetic rivalry, the Goroka Show has evolved into the largest cultural assembly in the South Pacific. Held annually in the Eastern Highlands Province of Papua New Guinea, this magnificent *sing-sing* brings together over one hundred indigenous groups from some of the most linguistically diverse valleys on Earth.\n\nThe aesthetic presentation of each clan represents an encyclopedic manifestation of Highland cosmology and social status. Dancers spend weeks preparing their *bilas* (ceremonial adornment). Towering headdresses reaching two meters high are meticulously assembled using the iridescent plumes of the Raggiana bird-of-paradise, the blue bird-of-paradise, and the black crests of the cassowary, anchored by prized mother-of-pearl kina shells. Facial ornamentation utilizes natural pigments: crushed ochre, charcoal, river clays, and tree sap, creating intricate geometric motifs that distinguish each lineage.\n\n![Carved wooden kundu hourglass drum with monitor lizard skin head at Sing-Sing](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/goroka-show-sing-sing-papua-new-guinea-detail.webp)\n\nThe acoustic landscape is anchored by the synchronized thrum of hundreds of hand-held *kundu* drums. Carved from solid hardwood in an hourglass profile and topped with monitor lizard skin secured by beeswax nodules to modulate pitch, the drums accompany chanting that reverberates across the arena. Alongside the vibrant dancers, the enigmatic Asaro Mudmen—encased in grey river sludge and wielding elongated bamboo finger-talons—re-enact legendary ancestral deceptions. The Goroka Show stands as a testament to the peaceful celebration of cultural sovereignty in the post-colonial Pacific."
