      "title": "Il Palio di Siena: Bareback Fury and Contrada Passion in Piazza del Campo",
      "summary": "Twice each summer, the medieval shell-shaped Piazza del Campo in Siena, Tuscany, is packed with passionate citizens as ten horses race bareback around the treacherous clay track for neighborhood honor.",
      "content": "![Horses and jockeys racing bareback at full speed on clay track in Piazza del Campo](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/palio-di-siena-piazza-del-campo-horse-race-hero.webp)\n\nDating back to the thirteenth century and refined into its modern format during the seventeenth century, the Palio di Siena is not merely an equestrian race, but the living civic pulse of this Tuscan hill town. Run twice annually—on July 2 in honor of the Madonna of Provenzano and on August 16 for the Assumption of Mary—the event pits ten of Siena's seventeen historical *contrade* against one another in an all-consuming passion that defines social identity from baptism to the grave.\n\nThe theatrical prologue unfolds as an authentic Renaissance masterpiece. In the afternoon, each competing horse (*barbero*) is brought directly into the local parish church to receive a priest's blessing (*Vai e torna vincitore!*). This is followed by the *Corteo Storico*, a majestic pageant of over six hundred participants in historical velvet doublets, featuring the breathtaking acrobatics of the *alfieri*—flag-throwers who launch heraldic silk banners tens of meters into the Tuscan sky to the roll of muffled snare drums.\n\n![Renaissance flag-thrower launching heraldic silk contrada banner into the air](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/palio-di-siena-piazza-del-campo-horse-race-detail.webp)\n\nThe race itself is ninety seconds of raw, unvarnished ferocity. Ridden bareback around the treacherous clay (*tufo*) track laid over Piazza del Campo's sloping paving stones, the jockeys maneuver through the perilous ninety-degree bends of the San Martino and Casato turns. Alliances are made, bribes exchanged, and whips (*nerbi*) brandished without restraint. Crucially, victory belongs to the horse (*cavallo scosso*), which can triumph even if its rider is thrown, provided its headpiece (*spennacchiera*) crosses the finish line first. The victorious *contrada* erupts in tears of ecstatic delirium, parading the painted silk banner (*drappellone*) directly into the Cathedral to give thanks."
