      "summary": "On January 6, Greek coastal towns and Aegean island harbors celebrate the Blessing of the Waters (*Theophany*), where young swimmers plunge into icy winter seas to retrieve a tossed wooden cross.",
      "content": "![Greek Epiphany procession at the harbor with decorated fishing boats and blue Aegean sea](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/greek-epiphany-blessing-waters-cross-diving-hero.webp)\n\nCelebrated across Hellas and the Aegean archipelago on January 6th, *Theophania* (or *Ta Phota*, 'The Lights') marks the conclusion of the twelve days of Christmas with the solemn Blessing of the Waters (*Megas Agiasmos*). Rooted in Byzantine liturgical rubrics, the ceremony celebrates the sanctification of the maritime environment, banishing mischievous winter goblins (*kalikantzari*) back to the subterranean underworld.\n\nThe procession commences from the parish cathedral down to the harborfront, led by silver cherubim banners (*hexapteryga*), incense-bearing deacons, and municipal marching bands. Priests dressed in gold-embroidered vestments carry the wooden or brass blessing cross twined with fragrant sprigs of basil (*vasilikos*), an aromatic herb associated with the Holy Cross in Orthodox lore. The quayside brims with anticipation as fishing trawlers, ferryboats, and caiques blow their air horns in unison.\n\n![Swimmer holding the carved wooden blessing cross aloft in the chilly sea waves](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-04/images/greek-epiphany-blessing-waters-cross-diving-detail.webp)\n\nThe dramatic climax occurs when the hierarch chants the baptismal hymn (*En Iordani*) for the third time and hurls the cross into the frigid Aegean waters. Immediately, seasoned local divers leap from piers and rowboats into the winter sea, thrashing through the swell to retrieve the submerged emblem. The diver who secures the cross raises it aloft, kissed by the waves, before kissing the relic and presenting it to the priest. The triumphant swimmer is paraded through the island alleys, bearing the blessed cross from home to home to bestow vitality and protection upon sea and shore."
